===========================================================================
Title: GOBASIC v3.1 for desktop
Developer: Luyao Zou
Date: July 2014
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Feature Updated:
>> Fix the Doppler shift calculation in 'molsim.m'
   Previously, uniform Doppler shift was applied to all frequencies.
   However, the Doppler shift is propotional to center frequency. The
   deviation (~1 MHz) in CSO band is negligible because the frequency
   resolution of CSO band is 1 MHz. However, the deviation will be much
   larger at HIFI band 5 (~ 1 THz).
   Now Doppler shift of each molecular transition is calculated based on
   the shift in velocity space and the line center frequency.


===========================================================================
Title: GOBASIC v3.0 for desktop
Developer: Luyao Zou
Date: June 2014
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Feature Updated:
>> Fix the problem of not finding global minimum by changing initial guess
   of column density and temperature to their logrithm.
>> Significantly improve the speed of spectra simulation by vectorization.
>> Add tutorial documents

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Major changes in codes:
>> optest.m
   Now column density is in its log10 value, and temperature is in its
   natural logrithm value.
   After optimization (line 51-52), convert the log values back to natural
   values before output.

>> jlabel_gen.m
   New file. Use to generate a label matrix for molsim()

>> molsim.m
   MAJOR improvement in code! Replace for loop by matrix operations.
   The basic idea is to use sparse matrix opteration to generate a matrix
   containing all gaussian lineshapes in the observation frequency range,
   then sum them up all together to create the simulated spectra.
   Three matrices are used: ilabel, jlabel and svalue, corresponding to
   the three vector elemenents in MATLAB sparse matrix generation function
   sparse(i,j,s).
   jlabel is created from 'jlabel_gen.m'. Each column of jlabel represents
   one molecular transition frequency (taken from 'molfreq'), and each
   element stores a matrix index of a 'obsfreq' frequency where the
   gaussian value is calculated. Only a certain 'range' of frequencies +/-
   line center is calculated (usu ~10 \sigma), because outside range the
   value is so close to 0 that calculation is unnecessary.

   ilabel creates the numbering of molecular transitions
   svalue() calculates the gaussian value.
   Then ilabel, jlabel and svalue are used to create the sparse matrix,
   whose columns represent the molecular transitions and rows represent
   the intensity at all observation frequencies.
   The sparse matrix is then summed by column, which creates the sum of
   all molecular lines in the observation frequency range. This is the
   final simulated spectra.

   For more details, please read document file 'vectorization.pdf'

>> analysisscript.m
   Add lines to create jlabel for each molecular component.

>> definevars.m
   Add lines to specify 'range'


===========================================================================
Title: GOBASIC v2.1 for Emerson Cluster
Developer: Luyao Zou
Date: Mar. 2014
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Feature Updated:
>> Fix a stupid mistake in calling the wrong columns in the catalog files
   that causes fitting problems
>> Ready to use on Emerson Center
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run on Emerson Center:
>> Log on to 'star' cluster
>> Modify 'definevars.m' 'optest.m' 'anonfun4.m' accordingly.
   !! Do make sure everything is correct! Catalogs and Results should be
   put into independent shared folders.
>> In the command line, type in 'mcc -mv Run.m'. The command line will
   compile the matlab files into executable c files.
>> Modify 'llq.cmd' load leveler file. Specify your directory.
>> Log on to 'euch4e' cluster, cd to your Gobasic directory, and type in
   'llsubmit llq.cmd' to submit the job
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Check List Before Compiling:
>> ALWAYS use relative directories
>> definevars.m
  * change 'obspath'
  * change 'source' size if is known. Otherwise type in 0
  * change 'appeff' and 'beam' size if necessary
  * change frequency window if necessary
  * change 'molpath1-4' for catalog files
  * change 'parpath1-4' for partition function files
  * change 'resultpath' and 'trialname' for output
  * include previous fittings if necessary
>> anofunc4.m
  * choose correct component numbers by comment out the other lines
   (end of the code)
  * include previous fittings if necessary
>> optest.m
  * change initial guess matrix and low, up limits
  * change 'MaxIter', 'MaxFunEvals', 'TolMesh', etc options accordingly
   (ususally there is no need to change)
  * choose correct component numbers by comment out the other lines
   in 'fit*=sim*(x)' chunk and '???comp' chunk.

===========================================================================
Title: GOBASIC v2.0 Revision
Revisor: Luyao Zou
Date: Dec.2013-Jan.2014
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Feature Revised:
>> Include the ability to handle different source size for different
   components.
>> Include calibrated observation spectra in the output file.
>> Include a user-specified frequency window in "definevars.m" in case the
   user wants to cut off the observational data.
>> Change all fucntion and variable names to lowercase letters (and renamed
   a few) in order to avoid ambiguity in various operating systems.
>> Improve program efficiency in "functiontest" by vectorization.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Major changes in codes:
>> definevars.m
   The feature of handling different source and beam size for different
   components are realized by assigning specific numbers to matrices
   "source" and "beam". Both are 1*4 row vectors, whose components
   correspond to the source and beam sizes of each fit component. The beam
   dilution factor B=source^2/(source^2+beam^2) are calculated later in the
   program to calibrate the fitting data for each component. The programs
   is designed so that if source=0 or source>beam, B is automatically set
   to 1, which means no beam dilution effect is accounted.

>> analysisscript.m
   Delete "source" and "beam" arguments in DatCal. Since the beam dilution
   effect is accounted in the fitting data, there is no more need to put
   these parameters in the calibration of observation data. Also this is
   the way it should be treated, since the observational data should only
   be calibrated by apperture efficiency and should not be rescaled further

>> spectrumcalibrate.m
   Delete "source" and "beam" arguments in this function

>> anonfun4.m
   Add lines to calculate the vector of beam dilution factor.

>> totalsim.m
   Add argument "beamdilution" to the function in order to calibrate
   fitting data.

>> maxtempcalc.m
   Add argument "beamdilution" to the function, and calibrate the fitting
   data.

>> onecomp.m  twocomp.m  threecomp.m  fourcomp.m
   Add "obsint" to "Fit" matrix to include observation data into the output
   inventory.

>> optest.m
   CO lines are flagged, removed from "obsint" and stored seperately in
   "co" before fitting. The final output file will add "co" back to the
   observation spectrum.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The fitting process follows the previous version.
First, edit definevars.m, optest.m and anonfun4.m
Second, run "functiontest" in the command line
Third, run "optest" in the command line

Major changes in input method:
>> Tab delimited catalog files can be stored in a specific folder, in which
   case the perl codes can be skipped. The default of perl codes is OFF.

>> Individual source and beam size for each component should be specified
   in "definevars.m"

>> The second column in the final output .csv file is the calibrated
   observation spectrum, including CO lines.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
>> To generate a simulated observation spectrum, run the following codes in
   command line

************************
freq = (FREQ_MIN:STEP:FREQ_MAX)';  % define a frequency window with desired
% step interval (in MHz). Type in specific numbers.
dlmwrite('filepath.csv',horzcat(freq,zeros(size(freq))),'precision',10);
% write data to file. The 'filepath.csv' is the filepath of the simulated
% observation spectrum.
% Go to "definevars.m" and assign "obspath"  with 'filepath', and assign
% the rest directories accordingly.
functiontest
simdata =  totalsim(molcal1(:,1),data(:,1),agulist1,a1,eup_j1,NT,FWHM,...
TEMP,SHIFT,BEAMDILUTION) % + totalsim(...) + ...
% NT,FWHM,TEMP,SHIFT,BEAMDILUTION are the user specified arguments. One
% "totalsim()" function generates one simulated component. If multiple
% components are needed, add more "totalsim()" with the same syntax and
% different user specified argument values.
dlmwrite('filepath.csv',[data(:,1) simdata],'precision',10);
*************************

Now a simulated spectrum should be saved to "filepath.csv"


===========================================================================
Title: GOBASIC
Authors: Mary Radhuber
===========================================================================
Code names: Agu.m, AList.m, AnalysisScript.m, anonfun4.m, ConstCalc.m,
            DefineVars.m, delimitcatalogfiles.pl, EinsteinAFind.m, ELJ.m,
            ELOFreq.m, ErrorBack.m, ErrorForward.m, EUJ.m, EUPFreq.m,
            fourcomp.m, FunctionTest.m, LimitData.m, LogInt.m,
            MaxTempCalc.m, molSim.m, onecomp.m, optest.m,
            PartitionFunction.m, PushResults.m, RenameandKill.m,
            SpectrumCalibrate.m, threecomp.m, totalSim.m, twocomp.m

Language: MATLAB

Code tested under the following compilers/operating systems:
Windows MATLAB 7.14, MATLAB 8, MATLAB 8.1, Linux MATLAB 8.1

Description of input data: Formatted partition function file and .cat file
in standard JPL format.  Observational data file.  Self absorption file.

Description of output data: best fit parameters and .csv formatted fit
simulation

System requirements: MATLAB and Active Perl must be installed.

Calls to external routines: none

Additional comments: Catalog files must be formatted before analysis
program can be run, however there is a script in the program called to do
this. The .par file must be formatted by hand.  Example file provided as
and Molecule.par. Catalog and .par files should be placed in the GOBASIC
folder.

The following files must be edited to run a new fit: DefineVars.m,
optest.m, and anonfun4.m

DefineVars.m:
line 5 'obspath' - string pointing to the observational data comma
                   delimited file
line 7 'AppEff' - apperture efficiency of the telescope used
line 9 'Source' - the source size in arcsec, or 0 if unknown
line 11 'Beam' - the beam size in arcsec
line 13 'threshold' - noise threshold in K, usually from 3-sigma rms noise
lines 16-19 'molpath1', etc. - strings pointing to the molecules to be fit.
                               All four paths must be defined even if less
                               than four components will be fit
lines 29-33 'parpath1', etc. - strings pointing to the .par data of the
                               molecules to be fit.  All four paths must be
                               defined for the same molecules in the same
                               order as 'molpath'.
line 35 'resultpath' - string to where the results file should be printed.
                       The folder specified must exist before the
                       optimization is run or the 'PushResults' function
                       will fail.
line 36 'trialname' - Enter the name of the molecule or the trial to be
                      run.  The 'PushResults' file will output the fit file
                      as resultspath\trialname.csv
lines 39-50 'S02Path", etc. - change paths to where the PushResults
                              function has pushed the previous fit results.
                              Leave molecules that haven't previously been
                              fit commented out.  Additional molecules can
                              be added to this list if the proscribed
                              format is followed.  This will also require
                              adding the molecule to the anonfun4 file.

optest.m:
lines 5-16 'low', 'up', and 'init' - low is the lower limit of all fit
                                     parameters, 'up' is the upper limit of
                                     all fit parameters, 'init' is the
                                     initial position of the fit.  All
                                     values must be defined, low must be
                                     less than up or the pattern search fit
                                     will throw an error and 'init' must be
                                     in the range of (low,up) or the
                                     minimum of the function in this range
                                     will not be found.

lines 29-32 - only the desired number of fits should be left uncommented.
              Exactly one option must be left uncommented.

anonfun4.m lines 9-19 - molecule fits must be uncommented according to
                        which previous fits are desired to be included.
                        Additional molecules can be added as long as the
                        proscribed format is followed.
lines 48-51 - only the desired number of fits should be left uncommented.
              Exactly one option must be left uncommented.

Once the changes have been made to the files the user can run the program
by running the 'FunctionTest' command in the MATLAB command prompt.
This will format all of the files, import the observational data, molecular
data, and previous fit data.  If any changes are made to the 'DefineVars.m'
file the 'FunctionTest' command must be run again.  To begin a fit the user
runs the 'optest' command.  If the user desires to change the number of
components used in a fit without changing which molecules are defined for
those components in 'DefineVars.m' the 'optest.m' and 'anonfun.m' files can
be edited and then 'optest' can be run again without needing to run
'FunctionTest' an additional time.

If the user wants to create a synthetic spectrum in the range of the
observational data they can use the 'totalsim' command.  The user should
type in the command line:
'totalsim(DatCal(:,1),DatCal(:,end),AguList,A,EUJ,NT,FWHM,Temp,Shift)'
DatCal(:,1),DatCal(:,end),AguList,A, and EUJ are all calculated by the
program so the user should type these exactly.  The NT,FWHM,Temp, and Shift
are the user specified parameters.

===========================================================================
The AAS gives permission to anyone who wishes to use these subroutines
to run their own calculations.  Permission to republish or reuse these
routines should be directed to the Executive Officer of the AAS.

Note that the AAS does not take responsibility for the content of the
source code.  Potential users should be wary of applying the code
to conditions that the code was not written to model and the accuracy of
the code may be affected when compiled and executed on different systems.
